CAT = "ukcareSafety"; SEG = "HSI"
# Standard 13 Health and safety, Standard 15 Infection prevention and control
Q = [
("Which law sets out the main duties of employers and employees for health and safety at work in Great Britain?",
 "The Health and Safety at Work etc. Act 1974", ["The Care Act 2014", "The Health and Social Care Act 2008", "The Workplace Regulations 1992"],
 "The Health and Safety at Work etc. Act 1974 places duties on employers to protect staff and others, and on employees to take reasonable care and cooperate."),
("What is one of your own duties as an employee under health and safety law?",
 "Take reasonable care of others", ["Write the risk assessments for the home", "Buy your own protective equipment", "Inspect the lifting equipment yearly"],
 "Employees must take reasonable care of their own health and safety and that of others, cooperate with their employer and use equipment as trained."),
("What is the correct order for managing a manual handling task?",
 "Avoid, assess, reduce", ["Lift, carry, lower", "Reduce, report, repeat", "Assess, lift, record"],
 "Under the Manual Handling Operations Regulations 1992 employers should avoid hazardous manual handling where possible, assess what cannot be avoided and reduce the risk."),
("Before using a hoist, what should you check?",
 "The sling and hoist are right and safe", ["Only that the battery light is on", "That a relative is there to watch", "That the person has eaten recently"],
 "Check you are trained, the care plan says which hoist and sling to use, the equipment is in date for inspection and undamaged, and the person consents."),
("Which regulations require hoists used for lifting people to be thoroughly examined at set intervals?",
 "LOLER 1998", ["COSHH 2002", "RIDDOR 2013", "UK GDPR"],
 "The Lifting Operations and Lifting Equipment Regulations 1998 (LOLER) require thorough examination of lifting equipment; equipment for lifting people is examined at least every six months."),
("What does COSHH cover?",
 "Substances that harm health", ["Reporting injuries at work", "Lifting people safely", "Storing medicines at home"],
 "The Control of Substances Hazardous to Health Regulations 2002 cover substances such as cleaning chemicals. Use and store them as the data sheet and risk assessment say."),
("What does RIDDOR require?",
 "Reporting certain injuries", ["Wearing gloves for all care", "Checking fire doors daily", "Recording all meals eaten"],
 "The Reporting of Injuries, Diseases and Dangerous Occurrences Regulations 2013 require employers to report specified injuries, diseases and dangerous occurrences to the enforcing authority."),
("You find a chemical in an unlabelled bottle. What should you do?",
 "Do not use it and report it", ["Smell it to identify it", "Pour it down the sink", "Use it if it looks clean"],
 "Unlabelled substances could be dangerous. Do not use or sniff them; report to your manager so they can be identified and disposed of safely."),
("Which fire extinguisher is suitable for an electrical fire?",
 "Carbon dioxide (black label)", ["Water (red label)", "Foam spray (cream label)", "Wet chemical (yellow label)"],
 "Carbon dioxide extinguishers, with a black label, are suitable for electrical fires. Water must never be used on live electrical equipment."),
("What should you do first if you discover a fire at work?",
 "Raise the alarm", ["Finish your current task", "Collect your belongings", "Open all the windows"],
 "Raise the alarm so everyone is warned and help is called, then follow your fire procedure. Only tackle a fire if trained and it is safe."),
("Which of these is a sign of stress in a care worker?",
 "Poor sleep and irritability", ["Enjoying time with friends", "Taking regular breaks", "Feeling calm before a shift"],
 "Stress can show as poor sleep, irritability, tiredness, headaches and difficulty concentrating. Spotting your own triggers helps you seek support early."),
("Where can a care worker get support with work-related stress?",
 "Their manager or a support line", ["The people they care for", "An anonymous social media group", "Nowhere, it is part of the job"],
 "Talk to your manager, use supervision and any employee assistance service, and see your GP if needed. Stress affects safety as well as wellbeing."),
("Which link comes first in the six-link chain of infection?",
 "The infectious agent", ["The susceptible host", "The portal of entry", "The mode of transmission"],
 "The chain runs: infectious agent, reservoir, portal of exit, mode of transmission, portal of entry, susceptible host. Breaking any link stops the spread."),
("What is the single most important way to prevent the spread of infection?",
 "Good hand hygiene", ["Opening windows daily", "Wearing a uniform", "Taking vitamins"],
 "Hand hygiene at the right moments is the most effective way to prevent infections spreading between people."),
("How long should a thorough handwash with soap and water take?",
 "40 to 60 seconds", ["5 to 10 seconds", "About 15 seconds", "Over 3 minutes"],
 "A full handwash takes about 40 to 60 seconds, covering all parts of the hands. An alcohol hand rub takes about 20 to 30 seconds."),
("When is alcohol hand rub not enough, so you must wash with soap and water?",
 "After care for someone with diarrhoea", ["After touching a clean table top", "Before making a phone call", "After filling in a care chart"],
 "Alcohol rub does not work well against Clostridioides difficile spores or norovirus, and does not clean visibly dirty hands. Use soap and water."),
("In what order should you put on an apron, mask and gloves?",
 "Apron, then mask, then gloves", ["Gloves, then mask, then apron", "Mask, then gloves, then apron", "Gloves, then apron, then mask"],
 "Put on the apron first, then the mask or respirator and eye protection, and gloves last. Gloves are removed first because they are the most contaminated."),
("Which item of PPE should you take off first?",
 "Gloves", ["Apron", "Mask", "Eye protection"],
 "Gloves are the most contaminated, so they come off first, then the apron, then eye protection and finally the mask. Clean your hands afterwards."),
("Why should you not wear a wristwatch or long sleeves during personal care?",
 "Germs collect on them", ["They are too expensive to risk", "They distract the person", "They break PPE rules on colour"],
 "Being bare below the elbows lets you wash hands and wrists properly and stops germs being carried on cuffs and jewellery."),
("How should you deal with a small blood spill in a care home?",
 "Wear PPE and use the spill kit", ["Wipe it with a dry paper towel", "Cover it and leave it for later", "Mop it up with the floor mop"],
 "Follow the spill procedure: wear gloves and an apron, use the spill kit or the disinfectant your policy sets, then dispose of waste as clinical waste and clean your hands."),
("What should you do with a used needle?",
 "Put it straight in a sharps bin", ["Recap it before putting it away", "Put it in a clinical waste bag", "Leave it on the tray for a nurse"],
 "Dispose of sharps immediately at the point of use into a sharps container, without recapping. Do not fill the bin above the fill line."),
("What should you do straight away after a needlestick injury?",
 "Let it bleed and wash it", ["Suck the wound to clean it", "Scrub it with a nail brush", "Cover it and carry on working"],
 "Encourage it to bleed without sucking it, wash with running water and soap, cover it, then report it urgently so you can be assessed."),
("How should linen soiled with body fluids be handled?",
 "In a dissolvable bag in a red bag", ["Shaken out before bagging it", "Rinsed by hand in the sink first", "Washed with other laundry"],
 "Infectious or soiled linen goes in a water-soluble (alginate) bag inside an outer bag, usually red, so it can go into the washer without being handled again. Never shake it."),
("Which of these breaks the chain of infection at the portal of entry?",
 "Covering a cut with a dressing", ["Putting soiled waste in a bin", "Cleaning a commode after use", "Isolating a sick resident"],
 "Covering broken skin stops germs getting in. Cleaning equipment removes the reservoir, and isolation reduces transmission."),
("What is a healthcare-associated infection?",
 "An infection linked to care", ["An infection a person had at birth", "An infection only staff can catch", "An infection caught on holiday"],
 "A healthcare-associated infection develops as a result of care or treatment in any setting, such as a urine infection from a catheter."),
("Why is cleaning a commode after each use important?",
 "It removes germs", ["It makes it lighter to move", "It stops it being stolen", "It keeps it warm for use"],
 "Cleaning and disinfecting shared equipment between uses removes the reservoir of germs and stops infections spreading between people."),
("You have diarrhoea and vomiting before a shift. What should you do?",
 "Stay off and tell your manager", ["Work but wear gloves all day", "Work if you feel well enough", "Take medicine and go in anyway"],
 "Staff with diarrhoea or vomiting should stay away from work until symptom free for 48 hours, as set out in infection control guidance, to avoid spreading illness."),
("Which waste usually goes in an orange clinical waste bag?",
 "Infectious clinical waste", ["Food waste from the kitchen", "Paper from the office", "Empty drink cans"],
 "Orange bags are for infectious clinical waste. Bag colours and rules depend on the setting, so follow your waste policy."),
]
